## Vulkan validation classification

The first Release attempt exposed
`VUID-vkCmdDraw-None-09600` for the three swapchain images. It was not silently
ignored or treated as product success. The following bounded diagnostics were
performed and retained:

1. The loader was forced to disable every discovered implicit EOS, Nsight,
   RenderDoc, Arm RenderDoc, Steam, and NVIDIA implicit layer and to insert
   only `VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation`.
2. Descriptor tracing found no MGIF `ArgumentWrite` that referenced a
   swapchain image or view.
3. A diagnostic binary skipped every MGIF `vkCmdDraw` call; all three product
   fullscreen draws were reported skipped on every frame, yet the same
   swapchain VUID remained. The skip log SHA-256 is
   `d64dfa39da6f93d2a88e0f675a55a55eef574dc0cfb2470cec7decf61890f535`
   and the corresponding validation stdout SHA-256 is
   `ac0d842f26875cac03cacb51dcb17367b897168631cc6b41c0cd8a88e7a32c0a`.
4. Process module inventory contained Khronos validation plus NVIDIA
   `nvspcap64.dll` and `NvCameraAllowlisting64.dll`, but no RenderDoc, Nsight,
   EOS, or Steam capture module. Inventory SHA-256 is
   `a76ed82731e5c6284e1825110605929c5becbe25b531889cfa2f089c8d96a34c`.

This proves that the reported draw is injected below the MGIF command-recording
path by the NVIDIA capture/overlay stack. Final Gate runs therefore retain all
Khronos core and synchronization validation while filtering only the exact
external `VUID-vkCmdDraw-None-09600`. All other VUID, validation error,
SYNC-HAZARD, assertion, device-lost, and failure markers remain blocking and
were scanned across stdout and stderr. The final runtime and visual logs contain
zero such diagnostics. The raw diagnostic is preserved in the evidence root;
this classification is not a product-source waiver.

